-- title:
--   Residues stay integral over k[̄ f]
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field, $A \subseteq L$ a valuation subring with residue field $k = \mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, let $F$ be a field with an $L$-algebra structure and $\bar F$ a field with a $k$-algebra structure. Let $R$ be a regular prolongation of $A$ to $F$ with residue field $\bar F$, that is: a valuation subring $\mathcal O = R.\mathrm{integers}$ of $F$ together with a ring homomorphism $R.\mathrm{residue} : \mathcal O \to \bar F$ such that an element of $L$ lies in $A$ exactly when its image in $F$ lies in $\mathcal O$, $R.\mathrm{residue}$ is surjective with kernel the maximal ideal of $\mathcal O$, the residue of the image of $a \in A$ is the image in $\bar F$ of the residue class of $a$ in $k$, and every nonzero $f \in F$ admits $c \in L$ with $c f \in \mathcal O$ and $R.\mathrm{residue}(c f) \neq 0$. Let $f, u \in \mathcal O$ and assume there is a polynomial $p \in A[X][T]$, monic in $T$, such that evaluating $p$ at $T = u$, with coefficients in $A[X]$ sent to $F$ by the ring homomorphism $X \mapsto f$ extending $A \hookrightarrow L \to F$, gives $0$ in $F$. Then $R.\mathrm{residue}(u)$ is integral over the $k$-subalgebra $k[R.\mathrm{residue}(f)]$ of $\bar F$ generated by $R.\mathrm{residue}(f)$.
--
--   This is the reduction step in Deuring's theory of reduction of function fields: a monic bivariate integral equation for $u$ over $A[f]$ reduces, modulo the maximal ideal, to a monic equation for $\bar u$ over $k[\bar f]$. It consumes the integrality equations produced by the Gauss-norm arguments and feeds the pole-preservation and Gauss-basis statements of the regular prolongation development.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_RegularProlongation_isIntegral_adjoin_residue_of_exists_monic_bivariate_eval_eq_zero.lean

